WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.679 --> 00:00:06.279
In early 2017, Tomb Raider fans everywhere
got their first look at Swedish actress Alicia

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:10.940
Vikander as the series' iconic hero Lara Croft
in the upcoming film franchise reboot.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.990
But while Tomb Raider is Vikander's latest
big budget action role, the star has been

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:19.869
all over the place in the last decade, moving
from Swedish indie darling to A-list celebrity.

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:22.480
Here's a look at why the new Lara Croft looks
so familiar.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:23.880
Pure

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:29.660
One of the first films to land Alicia Vikander
some international recognition was 2009's

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:30.800
Swedish drama Pure.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.730
Directed by Lisa Langseth, the film stands
out as a real masterpiece of Swedish cinema,

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.020
landing Vikander 'Best Actress in a Leading
Role' honors at the Swedish version of the

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.140
Academy Awards and launching her on the path
to superstardom.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.060
A Royal Affair

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.800
Vikander followed up her breakthrough performance
in Pure with a supporting role opposite Mads

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:52.130
Mikkelsen in the 2012 historical drama A Royal
Affair.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.390
The critically acclaimed film earned 'Best
Foreign Language Film' nominations at both

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.320
the Oscars and Golden Globes.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.250
Which is ironic considering Danish was actually
a foreign language for Vikander as well.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.250
She told the Independent,

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.320
"I had to call my friend's mother, who is
half-Danish.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.320
She actually recorded all of the lines (in
Danish) on her iPhone and sent them to me

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.120
so I was able to practice on my own."

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:12.280
Anna Karenina

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:17.560
Vikander's debut in English-language cinema
came in Joe Wright's 2012 film adaptation

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.120
of Leo Tolstoy's famous novel Anna Karenina.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.940
Filming the Russian classic was quite an adventure
itself.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.020
Vikander told The Guardian,

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:29.260
"It was 40 below, we didn't have hot water
for five days and slept in a cabin, on a bench.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.710
On our call sheets it actually said: 'Beware
of wolves.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.509
They are known to attack lone humans.'

00:01:33.509 --> 00:01:37.560
We saw a wolf one time, and a bear, but there
were some very tough Russian security guys

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:42.020
who came along … It was one of the most
fantastic adventures I've ever had in my life.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.840
But I don't need to do it twice."

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:45.900
The Fifth Estate

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.100
Vikander's English-language acting career
started gathering steam in 2013, when she

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.910
landed a supporting role in Bill Condon's
WikiLeaks thriller The Fifth Estate opposite

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:56.290
Benedict Cumberbatch.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:57.140
She told GQ,

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:58.720
"I knew a lot about WikiLeaks already.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.500
One of the most famous documentaries about
it came out in Sweden before the war logs

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.400
were released, and Julian Assange was still
considered a very brave man."

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:08.000
Hotell

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.940
2013 also saw Alicia Vikander star in the
Swedish drama film Hotell, in which she plays

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:18.290
Erika, a mother struggling with postnatal
depression who finds comfort in hotel rooms.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:22.489
Vikander found herself attracted to the script
because she enjoys playing uncomfortable roles—and

00:02:22.489 --> 00:02:25.780
because she'd already worked with the film's
director, Lisa Langseth, on Pure.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.360
She told Refinery29,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.060
"I was really drawn to the script.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.590
Many women go through [postnatal depression]
if they have a family, but it's still a very

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.519
taboo subject; I've never really seen it portrayed
onscreen.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.020
I mean, I didn't even really know how it would
work just by reading the script…

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.680
I was quite amazed at how Lisa was able to
bring it all to the screen."

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:44.180
Ex Machina

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:50.200
2014 would prove to be a very big year for
Alicia Vikander, due in no small part to her

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.380
attention-getting turn in Alex Garland's Ex
Machina.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.939
In the sci-fi psychological thriller, Vikander
played Ava, a beautiful and dangerous robot

00:02:57.939 --> 00:03:02.779
powered by artificial intelligence—a role
that landed her a slew of award nominations.

00:03:02.779 --> 00:03:05.349
Vikander was happy for the change of pace,
telling IndieWire,

00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:08.580
"I just love those intimate, psychological
sci-fi films.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.900
Then this script came along and it's one of
the best scripts I've read.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:15.109
Normally you come in and work on a script
and Alex was very open to us to change it,

00:03:15.109 --> 00:03:17.129
but it was just a very finished product.

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:17.980
It's a page-turner."

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:19.680
Testament of Youth

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:24.600
Vikander's stunning performance in Ex Machina
was followed by her portrayal of writer Vera

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.960
Brittain in James Kent's film adaptation of
her World War One memoir, Testament of Youth.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.420
In an interview with Collider, Vikander discussed
the difficulties of playing a real-life character.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:37.799
"You want to… especially give justice to
her family and friends who actually still

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.700
live and who remember her, who knew her.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:43.380
That was probably the most nerve-wracking
thing to meet them in person and do that.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.600
But they were so nice and they were really
there to support."

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:47.580
Son of a Gun

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:53.220
Alicia Vikander's third film in 2014 was Julius
Avery's Australian crime thriller Son of a

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:53.740
Gun.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.720
Vikander told IndieWire she was hooked from
the first page.

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.780
"When I read the script it had such pace,
and then it turns into a heist thriller, so

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.840
I was very intrigued to see how this director,
who I thought was a very arthouse director,

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.360
would do something like that."

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:07.980
Seventh Son

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:13.260
Sergei Bodrov's fantasy adventure film Seventh
Son was the fourth high-profile release featuring

00:04:13.269 --> 00:04:18.180
Alicia Vikander to hit theaters in 2014, but
it was actually one of her very first gigs,

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:19.780
having been filmed several years earlier.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:21.579
Vikander told GoPride.com,

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.020
"This is how studios work with releasing movies.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:27.740
It was a big experience for me because it
was the first American film that I got."

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:29.620
The Man from U.N.C.L.E.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:34.780
In 2015, Vikander made the jump to blockbusters
with Guy Ritchie's re-imagining of the hit

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:37.220
'60s television series The Man from U.N.C.L.E.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.740
Having appeared primarily in indie or lower-budget
films, The Man from U.N.C.L.E. was a new experience

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:42.260
for Vikander.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:43.400
She told Vulture,

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.680
"I mean, they closed down the Spanish Steps
in Rome.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:46.580
I was like, 'Are you kidding?'

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:47.800
I couldn't believe it!

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:51.400
But that's what you're able to do when you
have those budgets, and it was a huge gift

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:52.620
to be able to experience that.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:56.599
I could walk out on the street and feel like
I'd time-traveled, with 200 extras and all

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.820
the shop frames changed to look like the '60s."

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:00.400
The Danish Girl

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:06.500
In 2015, Vikander took on what would become
her most famous role to date, as Danish painter

00:05:06.509 --> 00:05:11.479
Gerda Wegener in Tom Hooper's film adaptation
of David Ebershoff's transgender novel The

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:12.220
Danish Girl.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.289
Vikander won the Academy Award for Best Supporting
Actress, but while she was happy with the

00:05:16.289 --> 00:05:18.300
win, she didn't think it was really a big
deal.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:19.760
She told Vogue,

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:23.730
"I think that it's all just buzz, and if people
recognize the film and talk about the film,

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.800
then maybe that will bring audiences to see
our film.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.600
It's an important story to tell, so that's
all we want."

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:30.080
Burnt

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.580
Rounding out 2015, Vikander appeared in the
John Wells cooking drama Burnt, in which she

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:38.440
appears in a very brief role as Bradley Cooper's
ex-girlfriend.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.449
The film was a commercial and critical disappointment,
so maybe next time the producers will do the

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:45.460
smart thing and give Vikander more screen
time!

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:47.340
Jason Bourne

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:53.000
2016 saw Alicia Vikander continue her rise
to mainstream stardom with her portrayal of

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.900
CIA Cyber Ops Division head Heather Lee in
the fifth installment of the Bourne series,

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:57.860
Jason Bourne.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.460
It was a dream come true for Vikander, who
has long been a fan of the franchise.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:02.879
She told The Independent,

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.099
"I was a teenager when I saw the first film…

00:06:05.099 --> 00:06:09.750
Even if it is a popcorn franchise movie, it's
intriguing because it has elements of political

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:13.460
and social issues that you recognize and yet
is still very entertaining."

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:15.260
The Light Between Oceans

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:20.020
After playing a department head at the CIA,
Vikander returned to period fare, starring

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:23.110
in the 2016 romantic drama The Light Between
Oceans.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:28.330
Vikander's onscreen romance with co-star Michael
Fassbender immediately led to popular speculation

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:32.650
that they were having an offscreen romance,
although neither actor would confirm the rumors.

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:36.380
Since filming The Light Between Oceans, though,
the pair's private romance has been a hot

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:40.460
topic for tabloids, turning the stars into
Hollywood's newest 'it' couple.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.160
Tulip Fever

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:45.940
In 2017, Vikander returned to period drama
in Tulip Fever.

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:50.860
The movie was actually filmed back in 2014,
though, and as you might expect, the long

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:54.520
delay was due to the fact that the film apparently
just isn't very good.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:58.680
Still, with a full docket of high-profile
releases on the horizon, including that Tomb

00:06:58.689 --> 00:07:02.379
Raider reboot, we've only seen the start of
what promises to be a long and fascinating

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:02.900
film career.

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:04.300
Thanks for watching!

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:06.830
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:08.699
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

